1
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:19,166
- Shibusawa.
- Okay, I'll get straight to the point.

2
00:00:20,441 --> 00:00:21,789
You two are jobless.

3
00:00:21,789 --> 00:00:24,029
And you're just wasting your days away.

4
00:00:24,029 --> 00:00:25,777
- So...
- Let me take over here.

5
00:00:26,385 --> 00:00:31,525
Actually, we'd like to sign
employment contracts with you both.

6
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:34,097
[Employment Contract]
Mr. Hirose,

7
00:00:34,097 --> 00:00:37,833
your skills as a pattern maker
and your experience as a planner.

8
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:39,673
And

9
00:00:40,585 --> 00:00:41,837
Ms. Sasaoka,

10
00:00:41,837 --> 00:00:45,305
your knowledge and execution
as a character producer.

11
00:00:45,305 --> 00:00:47,035
For us,

12
00:00:47,717 --> 00:00:50,473
and for Tuesla,
would you lend us your skills?

13
00:00:50,473 --> 00:00:52,309
I'll do it. Absolutely.

14
00:00:52,309 --> 00:00:55,557
I was about to beg you to let me do it.

15
00:00:55,557 --> 00:00:57,321
So, the money...

16
00:00:57,321 --> 00:00:59,001
- That's not acceptable.
- That's not acceptable.

17
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:01,901
Operating by burdening others,

18
00:01:01,901 --> 00:01:03,557
or pushing the strain onto them...

19
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:05,073
Wrinkly?

20
00:01:05,073 --> 00:01:06,805
Wrinkly.

21
00:01:07,609 --> 00:01:09,409
Let me take over here. Sorry.

22
00:01:09,409 --> 00:01:13,097
We've decided that Seo Design
will no longer create works

23
00:01:13,097 --> 00:01:16,969
that rely on the goodwill
and passion of the people involved.

24
00:01:16,969 --> 00:01:18,745
So...

25
00:01:19,666 --> 00:01:23,166
In that case, we'll gladly accept.

26
00:01:23,166 --> 00:01:25,169
Welcome, Shibusawa.

27
00:01:25,169 --> 00:01:29,066
But being realistic here,
what's Seo Design's financial situation like?

28
00:01:29,066 --> 00:01:31,653
Well, we're not exactly flush with cash.

29
00:01:31,653 --> 00:01:33,625
Now, we're basically operating
on the earnings

30
00:01:33,625 --> 00:01:35,137
<i>from Cho-cho-cho-cho-cho-cho-cho
in The Republic of Textorian.</i>

31
00:01:35,137 --> 00:01:36,797
That's what's keeping us afloat.

32
00:01:36,797 --> 00:01:40,229
So if the people of Textorian get tired
of that character, is it game over?

33
00:01:40,229 --> 00:01:43,377
Because of that unstable situation,

34
00:01:43,377 --> 00:01:45,769
the positions we're offering
aren't for formal employees,

35
00:01:45,769 --> 00:01:48,537
but limited-term contracts.

36
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:50,841
Which will happen first?

37
00:01:50,841 --> 00:01:54,461
Tuesla becoming a hit,
or Seo Design's funds running dry?

38
00:01:54,461 --> 00:01:56,921
It's fine! It's fine!

39
00:01:56,921 --> 00:01:59,621
Look, just look at this.

40
00:01:59,621 --> 00:02:02,549
So cute! Insanely cute!

41
00:02:02,549 --> 00:02:05,329
Cute times 100 equals sales! Right?

42
00:02:05,329 --> 00:02:07,973
Please use bank transfers from now on.

43
00:02:07,973 --> 00:02:10,553
- Okay.
- This is great news.

44
00:02:13,041 --> 00:02:15,700
So this is... love?

45
00:02:24,617 --> 00:02:26,577
- Right.
- Huh?

46
00:02:26,577 --> 00:02:29,885
Huh? It just stopped all of a sudden.

47
00:02:29,885 --> 00:02:34,321
Oh, with all the lightning strikes lately,
it's been acting up.

48
00:02:34,321 --> 00:02:35,325
Right.

49
00:02:35,325 --> 00:02:36,709
Maybe you should try a different outlet?

50
00:02:36,709 --> 00:02:38,269
No, I just changed it this morning.

51
00:02:38,269 --> 00:02:41,105
- Right.
- Oh, the lights upstairs are out too.

52
00:02:41,105 --> 00:02:43,337
- Right.
- Do you smell something weird?

53
00:02:43,337 --> 00:02:45,721
Oh, sorry! I just changed
my pain-relief patch.

54
00:02:45,721 --> 00:02:47,537
- Huh?
- No, this smell doesn't seem like

55
00:02:47,537 --> 00:02:49,961
- a pain-relief patch.
- Right.

56
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:52,077
Then it must be the garlic chive salad
I had this morning.

57
00:02:52,077 --> 00:02:54,749
- No way.
- It stinks.

58
00:02:59,433 --> 00:03:02,417
- Here, Mr. Hirose, put that over there.
- Ouch, ouch, ouch.

59
00:03:02,417 --> 00:03:05,069
- Over here, please, Ms. Sasaoka.
- Sure.

60
00:03:05,069 --> 00:03:07,619
Ouch, ouch... That hurts, that hurts.

61
00:03:09,225 --> 00:03:11,133
So this is O-Con's...

62
00:03:13,105 --> 00:03:15,813
Are you really okay with this?
Using your home as an office?

63
00:03:15,813 --> 00:03:17,593
It's fine. It's fine.
It's just for a little while.

64
00:03:17,593 --> 00:03:21,166
Oh, I'll hide a key in the mailbox.
Use that when I'm out.

65
00:03:21,909 --> 00:03:23,937
Hey, Mr. Seo, when did you say
the electrician was coming?

66
00:03:23,937 --> 00:03:25,549
- The repair.
- So O-Con is here...

67
00:03:25,549 --> 00:03:28,481
The repair, Mr. Seo.
The repair, the repair, the repair.

68
00:03:28,481 --> 00:03:32,700
Oh, well... I think he said in two weeks.

69
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:35,621
You've been acting
even more eccentric than usual lately.

70
00:03:35,621 --> 00:03:37,845
Are you feeling unwell?

71
00:03:45,629 --> 00:03:47,421
Not at all.

72
00:03:50,466 --> 00:03:51,601
Your ex-boyfriend...

73
00:03:51,601 --> 00:03:53,469
No, no, that's not what it is at all.

74
00:03:53,469 --> 00:03:55,197
- No, it's not.
- Lingering feelings?

75
00:03:55,197 --> 00:03:57,565
- Feelings? Feelings?
- It's not lingering feelings. Look.

76
00:03:57,565 --> 00:03:59,866
You can't just throw away plushies, right?

77
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:02,900
He's doing his best, so I'm thinking
I should too. That's all.

78
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,966
- That's totally lingering feelings.
- It's not!

79
00:04:04,966 --> 00:04:07,500
Hey!

80
00:04:08,577 --> 00:04:11,425
It'll get in the way of the design work.

81
00:04:16,541 --> 00:04:19,529
All right, let's get on with it.
Come on, hurry up.

82
00:04:19,529 --> 00:04:21,633
So this is what she likes.

83
00:04:26,125 --> 00:04:27,129
[Character Business Revolution]

84
00:04:27,129 --> 00:04:29,709
So that's the plan.

85
00:04:29,709 --> 00:04:33,437
Basically, just bam! Hit me with
your honest thoughts.

86
00:04:33,437 --> 00:04:34,901
Understood.

87
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:37,773
Then bam! I'll hit you with them.

88
00:04:45,133 --> 00:04:47,600
All right, I'm counting on you.

89
00:04:48,609 --> 00:04:50,841
- Harsh Review Molco is here!
- Thanks for having me here today.

90
00:04:50,841 --> 00:04:52,433
Thank you.

91
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:54,201
- Pleased to meet you.
- Pleased to meet you.

92
00:04:54,201 --> 00:04:56,029
Thank you.

93
00:04:56,029 --> 00:04:58,633
Hello, my surname is Megumi.

94
00:04:58,633 --> 00:05:00,489
It's a pleasure to meet you.

95
00:05:01,161 --> 00:05:04,113
Oh, hello. Sorry to
make you come all this way.

96
00:05:04,113 --> 00:05:06,989
Molco is joining the ranks of the stars.

97
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:14,065
Huh? I don't have anything for you right now.

98
00:05:14,065 --> 00:05:17,173
- What?
- Hit ideas don't just pop out that easily.

99
00:05:17,173 --> 00:05:18,177
No, no, no.

100
00:05:18,177 --> 00:05:21,097
As a character producer,
you took all that money.

101
00:05:21,097 --> 00:05:23,461
And you're like a dozing politician?
Just a salary thief.

102
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:25,829
- I mean...
- Excuse me, a vodka on the rocks.

103
00:05:25,829 --> 00:05:27,809
- Coming up.
- The thing is, looking back,

104
00:05:27,809 --> 00:05:29,345
the fact that Harsh Review Molco was launched

105
00:05:29,345 --> 00:05:32,133
against us on our release day
was seriously painful.

106
00:05:32,133 --> 00:05:34,769
Our main target demographics,
girls who love plushies,

107
00:05:34,769 --> 00:05:37,573
and kids, are all crazy for it.

108
00:05:37,573 --> 00:05:41,129
They're making a beeline
straight for Harsh Review Molco.

109
00:05:41,129 --> 00:05:44,149
They won't even glance your way?

110
00:05:44,149 --> 00:05:46,473
- That's exactly it.
- I see.

111
00:05:46,473 --> 00:05:49,463
Maybe we should just
find a blue ocean for Tuesla.

112
00:05:49,463 --> 00:05:52,549
That might be better.

113
00:05:52,549 --> 00:05:53,873
Blue?

114
00:05:53,873 --> 00:05:55,313
A blue ocean.

115
00:05:55,313 --> 00:05:58,389
It means an uncontested, untapped market.

116
00:05:58,389 --> 00:06:01,873
In other words,
finding a new target audience.

117
00:06:01,873 --> 00:06:03,265
But in the character business, at this point,

118
00:06:03,265 --> 00:06:05,385
a blue ocean is...

119
00:06:05,385 --> 00:06:07,729
Yo! Two!

120
00:06:07,729 --> 00:06:10,077
- Welcome!
- It's Mr. Yagi!

121
00:06:10,077 --> 00:06:11,133
Ready, set...

122
00:06:11,133 --> 00:06:14,905
- Yo! Two!
- Yo! Two!

123
00:06:14,905 --> 00:06:17,517
Hey! He's the one who made everyone
suspicious that we were plagiarizing!

124
00:06:17,517 --> 00:06:19,317
You ripped off our mascot, right?

125
00:06:19,317 --> 00:06:20,565
What?

126
00:06:20,565 --> 00:06:22,277
Huh? Why?

127
00:06:22,277 --> 00:06:24,481
What? You guys know him?

128
00:06:24,481 --> 00:06:27,129
Well... How do you know those two?

129
00:06:27,129 --> 00:06:30,213
I know him too.
I took some candid photos of him.

130
00:06:30,213 --> 00:06:32,253
- And...
- What? This is a miracle!

131
00:06:32,253 --> 00:06:36,933
- Ready, set... Yo! Two!
- Yo! Two!

132
00:06:42,245 --> 00:06:44,633
My grandma used to say that

133
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:50,033
someone who doesn't cry tears of blood
isn't truly sorry.

134
00:06:57,125 --> 00:07:00,166
Well... I can't cry blood or regular tears.

135
00:07:01,689 --> 00:07:03,429
But I am truly sorry.

136
00:07:03,429 --> 00:07:05,733
So how do you two know him?

137
00:07:05,733 --> 00:07:09,885
Oh, he happened to also go to
the same chiropractic clinic we go to.

138
00:07:09,885 --> 00:07:12,581
<i>Mr. Yagi was in so much back pain.</i>

139
00:07:12,581 --> 00:07:14,961
<i>His face was all twisted up
like a guardian lion dog.</i>

140
00:07:14,961 --> 00:07:16,785
<i>So we couldn't help but call out to him.</i>

141
00:07:16,785 --> 00:07:19,137
We asked him, "Which shrine do you guard?"

142
00:07:19,137 --> 00:07:21,125
And then we became friends.

143
00:07:21,125 --> 00:07:24,757
Stress-induced back pain
can be truly agonizing.

144
00:07:24,757 --> 00:07:28,961
But thanks to this little guy,
it's gotten a lot better.

145
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:30,325
Huh? Why do you have that?

146
00:07:30,325 --> 00:07:32,883
Well, he said he was struggling with stress,

147
00:07:32,883 --> 00:07:34,633
so I thought Tuesla would be perfect.

148
00:07:34,633 --> 00:07:37,977
But honestly, when I got it, I was confused.

149
00:07:37,977 --> 00:07:41,097
What's the point of having a plushie?

150
00:07:42,521 --> 00:07:46,453
But, but, once I started living with it,

151
00:07:46,453 --> 00:07:50,637
it's like my feelings got calmer,

152
00:07:50,637 --> 00:07:53,985
and I felt supported somehow.

153
00:07:53,985 --> 00:07:57,665
And so now, both my heart
and my back feel lighter.

154
00:07:57,665 --> 00:08:02,233
See? We're now comrades in loving Tuesla.

155
00:08:02,233 --> 00:08:07,453
Let's all have Zapaan Sours
and let bygones be bygones.

156
00:08:13,169 --> 00:08:16,566
I think I might have found it. A blue ocean.

157
00:08:18,861 --> 00:08:21,093
Tuesla's blue ocean is...

158
00:08:24,409 --> 00:08:26,633
the middle-aged corporate guy!

159
00:08:38,933 --> 00:08:40,573
[Episode 6]
Wow! How have you been?

160
00:08:40,573 --> 00:08:42,566
Oh, Kusakabe!

161
00:08:42,566 --> 00:08:45,000
Long time no see.
What's with the sudden visit?

162
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,701
Well, Mr. Dojima,
I heard you became the head of HR.

163
00:08:47,701 --> 00:08:50,745
Oh, are you coming back?
You'd be more than welcome.

164
00:08:50,745 --> 00:08:53,449
No, no, no... There's just
something I want to show you.

165
00:08:53,449 --> 00:08:55,013
This here.

166
00:08:55,013 --> 00:08:57,033
This is a plushie. Its name is Tutu-Tuesla.

167
00:08:57,033 --> 00:08:59,673
Wow, what's this all of a sudden?

168
00:08:59,673 --> 00:09:02,833
I heard you're struggling
with new hires quitting.

169
00:09:02,833 --> 00:09:06,253
So, here's this plushie.
It'll definitely be an asset to your company.

170
00:09:06,253 --> 00:09:09,833
First, please take a look at this data.

171
00:09:09,833 --> 00:09:12,309
It's a correlation chart between
organizational productivity and stress,

172
00:09:12,309 --> 00:09:14,445
published by the World Health Organization.

173
00:09:14,445 --> 00:09:15,745
[Productivity, Stress]
Take a look.

174
00:09:15,745 --> 00:09:17,766
Productivity here is on a downward trend.

175
00:09:17,766 --> 00:09:19,809
You really can't underestimate stress.

176
00:09:19,809 --> 00:09:22,889
- Ms. Kusakabe, what's up with you?
- That's where Tuesla comes in!

177
00:09:22,889 --> 00:09:26,221
Look at this form of stress relief.
The gentle touch.

178
00:09:26,221 --> 00:09:28,469
We've even heard from
executives at a major bank

179
00:09:28,469 --> 00:09:30,525
that it had a real positive effect.

180
00:09:30,525 --> 00:09:31,841
Hey, Ms. Kusakabe, what's gotten into you?

181
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:36,421
<i>♪ Tuesla, Tuesla, Tutu-Tuesla ♪</i>

182
00:09:36,421 --> 00:09:40,600
<i>♪ Mr. Dojima and Tutu-Tuesla ♪</i>

183
00:09:41,657 --> 00:09:44,773
I'm sorry, especially after you went to
the trouble of setting this up.

184
00:09:44,773 --> 00:09:46,865
Don't worry about that.

185
00:09:46,865 --> 00:09:48,257
But what's the reality?

186
00:09:48,257 --> 00:09:51,366
Plushies for middle-aged corporate guys?

187
00:09:52,697 --> 00:09:54,533
Yes, feeling great!

188
00:09:55,489 --> 00:09:58,965
- Want one too, Mr. Fuchinobe?
- Oh, no, I'm...

189
00:09:58,965 --> 00:10:00,977
The main target demographics
for the character business

190
00:10:00,977 --> 00:10:03,745
have long been girls and kids.

191
00:10:03,745 --> 00:10:06,969
But newcomers like us
can't really compete there.

192
00:10:06,969 --> 00:10:09,997
So we need to set sail for uncharted waters.

193
00:10:09,997 --> 00:10:12,341
It means...

194
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:15,221
Exactly! Middle-aged guys,
middle-aged guys, middle-aged guys!

195
00:10:15,221 --> 00:10:17,741
To all the stressed-out
working people out there,

196
00:10:17,741 --> 00:10:19,845
we give you Tutu-Tuesla!

197
00:10:19,845 --> 00:10:21,713
Tutu-Tuesla!

198
00:10:26,585 --> 00:10:30,633
Mr. Seo, have you finished
the promotional designs for Tuesla yet...

199
00:10:33,729 --> 00:10:36,966
Well, the promotional designs...

200
00:10:41,017 --> 00:10:42,989
Do you like her? Ms. Kusakabe, I mean.

201
00:10:42,989 --> 00:10:44,933
Oh, right, right.

202
00:10:46,141 --> 00:10:47,681
Huh?

203
00:10:47,681 --> 00:10:49,544
No, not at all! That "right, right"
just now wasn't a yes.

204
00:10:49,544 --> 00:10:51,845
It was more like the "sou, sou"
of flipping through an old album...

205
00:10:51,845 --> 00:10:53,193
- It's nothing...
- You don't have to hide it.

206
00:10:53,193 --> 00:10:54,929
I can tell just by watching you.

207
00:10:56,725 --> 00:10:58,877
Are you going to confess your feelings?

208
00:10:58,877 --> 00:11:00,800
Con...

209
00:11:02,485 --> 00:11:04,209
No.

210
00:11:05,173 --> 00:11:09,900
I don't totally get it,
but it's not exactly like that.

211
00:11:11,081 --> 00:11:13,291
I realized I have
that kind of feeling in me too.

212
00:11:13,291 --> 00:11:17,281
Once I understood that,
the cloudiness in my head cleared up,

213
00:11:17,281 --> 00:11:21,213
and I feel like I've become
strangely energized.

214
00:11:21,213 --> 00:11:24,106
So it's not like I want to
go out with her or anything.

215
00:11:26,941 --> 00:11:29,653
And more importantly,
this is a critical time right now.

216
00:11:29,653 --> 00:11:31,401
Welcome back, Ms. Kusakabe.

217
00:11:31,401 --> 00:11:34,213
Ah! Wait, wait, wait...

218
00:11:34,213 --> 00:11:35,913
I was lying.

219
00:11:39,209 --> 00:11:43,718
- I also think you shouldn't confess.
- What? Why?

220
00:11:43,718 --> 00:11:46,105
Because there's a big chance
you'll get rejected.

221
00:11:46,917 --> 00:11:48,925
Wait, is this still about that 'barley tea'?
[*Barley sounds like Mugi in Japanese]

222
00:11:48,925 --> 00:11:52,973
No, it's because Ms. Kusakabe
seems so happy right now.

223
00:11:52,973 --> 00:11:54,993
Well... So you mean...

224
00:11:54,993 --> 00:11:56,501
What do you mean?

225
00:11:56,501 --> 00:11:59,509
I think it's better not to
upset your current relationship.

226
00:12:01,225 --> 00:12:03,321
And if you still feel like
your feelings are going to overflow,

227
00:12:03,321 --> 00:12:05,429
please distract yourself with something else.

228
00:12:05,429 --> 00:12:10,325
Like eating sweets, exercising,
or trying to date me.

229
00:12:11,117 --> 00:12:14,533
Sweets, exercise, dating.

230
00:12:15,893 --> 00:12:17,621
Dating?

231
00:12:19,649 --> 00:12:22,885
I was lying. And by the way,
my recommendation is to focus on your core.

232
00:12:22,885 --> 00:12:25,603
Fix your posture,
focus your mind on your core,

233
00:12:25,603 --> 00:12:28,813
just below your navel, and breathe deeply.

234
00:12:30,025 --> 00:12:34,385
If you center your energy like this,
you can control those overflowing feelings.

235
00:12:40,957 --> 00:12:43,353
- Oh, good work today.
- Good work today.

236
00:12:43,353 --> 00:12:44,633
How was the sales pitch?

237
00:12:44,633 --> 00:12:48,149
Man, those middle-aged guys are tough.

238
00:12:48,149 --> 00:12:51,845
So far, zero interest.
But we just have to keep at it.

239
00:12:51,845 --> 00:12:53,237
See you.

240
00:12:53,237 --> 00:12:56,329
Oh, well... Just to be sure,
can I ask you something?

241
00:12:56,329 --> 00:12:58,429
- Oh, sure.
- Ms. Kusakabe,

242
00:12:58,429 --> 00:13:00,193
do you like Mr. Seo?

243
00:13:00,193 --> 00:13:02,177
Huh? Like Mr. Seo?

244
00:13:02,177 --> 00:13:07,393
Oh, no... I mean,
I've never really thought about it that way.

245
00:13:07,393 --> 00:13:11,829
He's a total weirdo,
an oddball, and eccentric,

246
00:13:11,829 --> 00:13:14,165
and can't do anything besides design, but...

247
00:13:14,165 --> 00:13:16,901
well, I like the things he makes.

248
00:13:16,901 --> 00:13:20,893
I mean, do you like him
as a romantic interest?

249
00:13:20,893 --> 00:13:25,526
Oh, no. That's a complete and total "no."

250
00:13:26,377 --> 00:13:28,265
Good.

251
00:13:28,265 --> 00:13:30,017
Alright, then.

252
00:13:32,845 --> 00:13:34,881
Good?

253
00:13:38,665 --> 00:13:43,466
Could it be that Ms. Sasaoka...

254
00:13:51,909 --> 00:13:54,181
Oh, my!

255
00:13:55,449 --> 00:13:58,266
This is for the promotion, right?

256
00:13:58,266 --> 00:14:00,625
Isn't this an insane amount?

257
00:14:00,625 --> 00:14:02,866
Oh, this is way too much "Oh, my!"

258
00:14:02,866 --> 00:14:06,893
An insane amount of these,
times "Oh, my!", equals "Ya!"

259
00:14:10,989 --> 00:14:12,777
Core.

260
00:14:12,777 --> 00:14:14,805
- Huh?
- Core.

261
00:14:14,805 --> 00:14:17,585
Core. Core, core, core...

262
00:14:17,585 --> 00:14:20,667
- Core, core, core?
- Core, core, core, core!

263
00:14:25,545 --> 00:14:27,289
Well, then.

264
00:14:37,553 --> 00:14:40,229
Good work.

265
00:14:40,229 --> 00:14:41,309
<i>You're right.</i>

266
00:14:41,309 --> 00:14:42,373
[Special Feature: Young Entrepreneur]
In the future,

267
00:14:42,373 --> 00:14:44,029
[Shindo and Harsh Review Molco]
I want to make it an ordinary reality

268
00:14:44,029 --> 00:14:46,413
[Exploring a Future]
for humanity to live in space.

269
00:14:46,413 --> 00:14:48,729
[Where Space Feels Closer]
So you see humanity's future in space?

270
00:14:48,729 --> 00:14:50,281
- Is that the idea?
- Yes.

271
00:14:50,281 --> 00:14:52,681
The time to graduate from
the cradle of Earth is now.

272
00:14:52,681 --> 00:14:54,401
In short?

273
00:14:54,401 --> 00:14:57,233
Oh, well, we're aiming for the next frontier.

274
00:14:57,233 --> 00:15:01,509
Even though Earth is piled high
with unsolved problems,

275
00:15:01,509 --> 00:15:04,633
all you can think about is space?

276
00:15:04,633 --> 00:15:07,969
<i>Oh, so in short, it's escapism.</i>

277
00:15:07,969 --> 00:15:10,953
This one... It's been getting
a bit of backlash online.

278
00:15:10,953 --> 00:15:13,085
In what way?

279
00:15:13,085 --> 00:15:15,237
[Merry Character Producer, Shiho Sato]

280
00:15:15,237 --> 00:15:19,385
I wish they'd stop this trend of
bashing people who talk about their dreams.

281
00:15:19,385 --> 00:15:21,953
Is this character a personification
of the reason why Japan

282
00:15:21,953 --> 00:15:23,697
has become a country that
can't take on challenges?

283
00:15:23,697 --> 00:15:27,341
Also, we've received a number of complaints
that kids have started

284
00:15:27,341 --> 00:15:30,661
imitating the phrase "in short."

285
00:15:33,921 --> 00:15:36,433
Harsh Review Molco...

286
00:15:39,737 --> 00:15:42,621
really stirred things up for us, right?

287
00:15:42,621 --> 00:15:47,329
Recent sales are the highest
since Merry was founded.

288
00:15:47,329 --> 00:15:49,269
Good work.

289
00:15:51,913 --> 00:15:57,013
We'll hold a press conference to apologize
and seal away Harsh Review Molco.

290
00:15:57,013 --> 00:15:58,861
Let's have Molco,

291
00:15:58,861 --> 00:16:00,917
now back as Cynical Molco,
hold the press conference.

292
00:16:00,917 --> 00:16:03,481
Set it up so it has lost its memories
from when it was Harsh Review Molco.

293
00:16:03,481 --> 00:16:06,589
That should work.

294
00:16:06,589 --> 00:16:09,601
Please make the arrangements
as soon as possible.

295
00:16:09,601 --> 00:16:10,629
Yes.

296
00:16:10,629 --> 00:16:13,269
All right, let's move on to
the next topic. Rena?

297
00:16:13,269 --> 00:16:14,757
Yes.

298
00:16:14,757 --> 00:16:17,717
This is the new character
we'll be announcing next.

299
00:16:17,717 --> 00:16:21,373
It travels on a journey to discover
whether it is Uiro or Suama.

300
00:16:21,373 --> 00:16:23,825
Sweet and plump.

301
00:16:23,825 --> 00:16:25,585
[New Character Proposal: Docchicchi]
Its name is Docchicchi.

302
00:16:25,585 --> 00:16:26,689
- Cute.
- Suama?

303
00:16:26,689 --> 00:16:27,897
- Docchicchi?
- Docchicchi?

304
00:16:27,897 --> 00:16:31,289
This gap after Harsh Review Molco
will definitely get people talking.

305
00:16:31,289 --> 00:16:32,621
Production of the plushies

306
00:16:32,621 --> 00:16:37,461
- is also going smoothly, so...
- Zapaan!

307
00:16:37,461 --> 00:16:41,566
It'd be great if we could just
smoothly transition to that.

308
00:16:42,565 --> 00:16:44,685
But who knows.

309
00:16:44,685 --> 00:16:47,701
What are you trying to say?

310
00:16:47,701 --> 00:16:52,133
Listen, characters are living things.

311
00:17:07,437 --> 00:17:12,425
Mr. Seo, that photo of Tuesla
looking like it's going to the office,

312
00:17:12,425 --> 00:17:17,233
I was thinking of posting it on social media,
but it's just not quite right.

313
00:17:18,509 --> 00:17:23,601
Core... core... core... core...

314
00:17:23,601 --> 00:17:28,021
Core... core... core...

315
00:17:28,021 --> 00:17:30,766
Core... core...

316
00:17:32,053 --> 00:17:33,897
Core.

317
00:17:38,109 --> 00:17:40,066
What is it?

318
00:17:41,661 --> 00:17:43,357
I'm busy, you know.

319
00:17:43,357 --> 00:17:45,362
I'm heading out to take care of
some sales-related stuff.

320
00:17:45,362 --> 00:17:47,777
Can you take over here? With Ms. Sasaoka.

321
00:17:47,777 --> 00:17:49,489
Okay, understood.

322
00:17:53,533 --> 00:17:55,089
Is something wrong?

323
00:17:55,089 --> 00:17:56,797
No, it's just this here...

324
00:17:56,797 --> 00:17:58,117
[Cynical Molco
Holds Apology Press Conference]

325
00:17:58,117 --> 00:17:59,441
[Controversial Harsh Review Molco
to Be Sealed Away]

326
00:17:59,441 --> 00:18:02,041
That's a very Omishima-like
way of handling things.

327
00:18:02,041 --> 00:18:04,033
I wonder if Merry will be okay.

328
00:18:04,637 --> 00:18:06,389
Wait, you two.

329
00:18:06,389 --> 00:18:10,157
We all left Merry totally pissed off,
and now you're feeling sorry for them?

330
00:18:10,157 --> 00:18:13,201
No, that's not it. It's just that
we still have friends there.

331
00:18:13,201 --> 00:18:15,500
What should we do, Molco?

332
00:18:16,425 --> 00:18:18,121
Should we fire those guys?

333
00:18:18,121 --> 00:18:19,189
But for you too, Mr. Seo,

334
00:18:19,189 --> 00:18:21,103
- isn't Merry a special place?
- No, no.

335
00:18:21,103 --> 00:18:23,093
Huh? Why? Why would I care about

336
00:18:23,093 --> 00:18:24,093
- a place like Merry...
- Okay, okay, okay!

337
00:18:24,093 --> 00:18:25,400
Enough! Stop it!

338
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:27,213
Oh, seriously.

339
00:18:28,933 --> 00:18:30,737
Yes! Here it is!

340
00:18:30,737 --> 00:18:33,785
- Huh?
- Really? Could it be...

341
00:18:33,785 --> 00:18:36,033
They want to hear about Tuesla!

342
00:18:39,665 --> 00:18:41,481
Tuesla! Tuesla!

343
00:18:41,481 --> 00:18:43,065
Tuesla! Tuesla!

344
00:18:43,065 --> 00:18:44,341
- Tuesla!
- Tu... My back...

345
00:18:44,341 --> 00:18:46,189
Tuesla! Tuesla!

346
00:18:46,189 --> 00:18:48,883
- Tuesla! Tuesla!
- Core. Core. Core.

347
00:18:51,213 --> 00:18:54,745
What kind of company is
"All Japan Heavy Industries"?

348
00:18:54,745 --> 00:18:56,866
Japan's largest steel manufacturer.

349
00:18:56,866 --> 00:19:00,617
A mega-corporation with
over 110,000 employees.

350
00:19:00,617 --> 00:19:03,977
Well, why would
such a heavy-duty company come to us?

351
00:19:03,977 --> 00:19:06,429
I cold-called a bunch of companies
that are focusing on stress management,

352
00:19:06,429 --> 00:19:08,733
and pitched to anyone who would listen.

353
00:19:08,733 --> 00:19:10,665
And they were one of them.

354
00:19:10,665 --> 00:19:15,033
But a plushie for this company is a bit...

355
00:19:16,281 --> 00:19:20,237
Hey, 110,000 middle-aged guys
is a huge opportunity.

356
00:19:20,237 --> 00:19:22,969
Seeing this dark gleam, it's a bit...

357
00:19:22,969 --> 00:19:25,453
No, no, this is Tuesla we're talking about.

358
00:19:25,453 --> 00:19:28,629
It'll be a breeze.
I'm practically skipping here.

359
00:19:30,841 --> 00:19:33,266
I envy him,
living in his own world like that.

360
00:19:40,401 --> 00:19:42,189
This way, please.

361
00:19:42,189 --> 00:19:43,841
Okay.

362
00:19:43,841 --> 00:19:45,900
Iron men.

363
00:19:47,585 --> 00:19:50,009
Mr. Seo. Mr. Seo, let's go.

364
00:19:51,181 --> 00:19:54,365
Are they really all going to be like that?

365
00:19:54,365 --> 00:19:56,600
110,000 iron men.

366
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:58,517
No, I doubt it... I mean,

367
00:19:58,517 --> 00:20:01,653
just because it's a steel manufacturer,
you can't stereotype them like that.

368
00:20:06,741 --> 00:20:09,041
[Mock a single yen,
and you'll cry over a single yen]

369
00:20:09,041 --> 00:20:11,141
[Thrift is the iron law]

370
00:20:15,853 --> 00:20:19,413
Our CEO, Mr. Hino,
will also be joining us today.

371
00:20:19,413 --> 00:20:21,473
He will be sitting in.

372
00:20:21,473 --> 00:20:22,989
It's a pleasure to meet you.

373
00:20:22,989 --> 00:20:25,500
- Pleased to meet you.
- Pleased to meet you.

374
00:20:28,373 --> 00:20:30,653
The iron president.

375
00:20:30,653 --> 00:20:35,266
All right then, may we hear
what you have to say?

376
00:20:39,153 --> 00:20:41,041
Feeling great.

377
00:20:43,633 --> 00:20:45,617
All right, regarding our company's product,

378
00:20:45,617 --> 00:20:49,500
the Tutu-Tuesla plushie,
please allow me to explain.

379
00:20:50,521 --> 00:20:52,689
Tuesla is a character from another planet,

380
00:20:52,689 --> 00:20:57,681
a lonely hyena, who came to Earth
to support people who have lost their energy.

381
00:20:57,681 --> 00:21:00,189
That's the character's backstory.

382
00:21:00,189 --> 00:21:04,405
And this is the plushie.

383
00:21:04,405 --> 00:21:06,877
It's not just a simple plushie.

384
00:21:07,537 --> 00:21:11,645
Through its adorable form
and pleasant texture,

385
00:21:11,645 --> 00:21:13,025
it provides mental care for workers,

386
00:21:13,025 --> 00:21:15,136
- while also...
- I have already reviewed everything

387
00:21:15,136 --> 00:21:19,873
written in these documents in advance.

388
00:21:19,873 --> 00:21:22,513
The reason we called you in here today

389
00:21:22,513 --> 00:21:26,057
is that we wanted to see the evidence.
[*Evidence sounds like shrimp in Japanese]

390
00:21:26,057 --> 00:21:28,465
- Shrimp?
- Our apologies.

391
00:21:28,465 --> 00:21:31,173
But at this stage, we don't yet have
a body of medical evidence

392
00:21:31,173 --> 00:21:33,549
that you could call solid proof.

393
00:21:37,045 --> 00:21:41,201
However, research has linked contact
with attachment objects like plushies

394
00:21:41,201 --> 00:21:43,901
to lower levels of
the stress hormone cortisol

395
00:21:43,901 --> 00:21:46,173
and increased secretion of oxytocin,

396
00:21:46,173 --> 00:21:48,281
which is associated
with feelings of security.

397
00:21:48,281 --> 00:21:49,781
Corti... Oxy...

398
00:21:49,781 --> 00:21:53,165
Therefore, it's reasonable to consider Tuesla

399
00:21:53,165 --> 00:21:54,961
as a supplementary tool
for stress management.

400
00:21:54,961 --> 00:21:57,109
By the way, what's your track record?

401
00:21:57,109 --> 00:21:59,300
We don't have a track record yet.

402
00:22:02,533 --> 00:22:08,593
Honestly, with just these materials,
it'd be difficult for us to sign a contract.

403
00:22:09,381 --> 00:22:12,405
Huh? Wait, wait, wait.

404
00:22:12,405 --> 00:22:15,469
I don't get it. So what
does that mean, in short?

405
00:22:15,469 --> 00:22:17,833
Let's have that be all for today.

406
00:22:17,833 --> 00:22:19,989
No, no, no. That's impossible.

407
00:22:19,989 --> 00:22:22,628
Next time, please prepare
new materials separately...

408
00:22:22,628 --> 00:22:24,585
No. That's not what I'm talking about.

409
00:22:24,585 --> 00:22:26,465
You haven't even touched it yet!

410
00:22:27,441 --> 00:22:29,969
- Touched...
- One look and your heart goes "zap!"

411
00:22:29,969 --> 00:22:32,769
One touch and it's "bam!" That's Tuesla!

412
00:22:32,769 --> 00:22:36,193
It's a plushie that's made to be
by someone's side!

413
00:22:36,193 --> 00:22:38,485
I don't want our fate to be decided

414
00:22:38,485 --> 00:22:41,125
by things like a track record
or a shrimp tempura bowl!

415
00:22:41,125 --> 00:22:42,801
Evidence.

416
00:22:42,801 --> 00:22:46,025
Right... That... Evi...

417
00:22:46,025 --> 00:22:48,586
I don't want that to decide this!

418
00:22:57,889 --> 00:23:01,166
Here. Just touch it, properly.

419
00:23:14,201 --> 00:23:17,333
All right, now hug it.

420
00:23:33,233 --> 00:23:35,193
This is ridiculous.

421
00:23:35,193 --> 00:23:37,293
Okay, I'll give it back to you now.

422
00:23:37,293 --> 00:23:39,229
That won't be necessary.

423
00:23:40,769 --> 00:23:42,509
We're giving them to you.

424
00:23:43,349 --> 00:23:46,001
You're saying we can take two?

425
00:23:46,001 --> 00:23:47,941
Two?

426
00:23:47,941 --> 00:23:50,437
Who said anything so stingy?

427
00:23:51,709 --> 00:23:55,053
I'm saying we'll give you
every single Tuesla we've produced,

428
00:23:55,053 --> 00:23:57,257
free of charge.

429
00:23:58,029 --> 00:24:00,633
However, there's a condition.

430
00:24:00,633 --> 00:24:04,177
Your company's management team

431
00:24:04,177 --> 00:24:08,753
must keep Tuesla on their person
at all times for one week.

432
00:24:08,753 --> 00:24:10,077
What a stupid idea.

433
00:24:10,077 --> 00:24:14,601
During desk work, meetings,
even meals. All the time.

434
00:24:14,601 --> 00:24:19,193
That's enough. A meeting
like this is a waste of time...

435
00:24:21,541 --> 00:24:25,201
And by doing that,
we'll understand this product's value?

436
00:24:26,277 --> 00:24:27,381
Yes.

437
00:24:27,381 --> 00:24:30,541
But giving them all away
for free is quite generous.

438
00:24:30,541 --> 00:24:32,729
No way.

439
00:24:32,729 --> 00:24:34,593
If, in one week, you don't come to us

440
00:24:34,593 --> 00:24:37,625
saying you absolutely want to buy 100 more,

441
00:24:37,625 --> 00:24:40,441
we won't be able to make our next plushie.

442
00:24:40,441 --> 00:24:44,801
We'll suspend operations and go bankrupt.

443
00:24:44,801 --> 00:24:48,489
Oh... So you don't have
any evidence or track record,

444
00:24:48,489 --> 00:24:50,357
but you're confident.

445
00:24:53,481 --> 00:24:57,033
Very well. We'll accept your terms.

446
00:24:57,033 --> 00:24:59,229
Starting now, for one week,
I'll instruct our management

447
00:24:59,229 --> 00:25:02,500
- to keep this plushie...
- It's Tuesla.

448
00:25:03,233 --> 00:25:08,200
To keep Tuesla on them.

449
00:25:09,369 --> 00:25:11,100
Thank you very much.

450
00:25:20,893 --> 00:25:21,957
<i>We did it.</i>

451
00:25:21,957 --> 00:25:23,065
How do you figure?

452
00:25:23,065 --> 00:25:24,961
How exactly did we "do it"?

453
00:25:24,961 --> 00:25:27,229
I mean, giving it all away
for free is a bit much.

454
00:25:27,229 --> 00:25:28,937
It's fine.

455
00:25:28,937 --> 00:25:30,966
Tuesla's goodness will definitely
get through to them.

456
00:25:30,966 --> 00:25:33,205
And then, additional orders!
100, 200, 300 units!

457
00:25:33,205 --> 00:25:35,397
No, that's way too optimistic.

458
00:25:35,397 --> 00:25:37,477
Hey, let me teach you a good phrase.

459
00:25:37,477 --> 00:25:39,187
A great person once said it.

460
00:25:39,187 --> 00:25:41,049
You can't stay a dreamy-eyed girl.

461
00:25:41,049 --> 00:25:43,797
Wait, do you have
some kind of basis for this?

462
00:25:43,797 --> 00:25:46,013
Actually, I asked a junior
from my consulting days

463
00:25:46,013 --> 00:25:47,437
about it beforehand.

464
00:25:47,437 --> 00:25:50,297
About what kind of person Mr. Hino is.

465
00:25:50,297 --> 00:25:53,257
<i>And they said he's called
"Blast Furnace Hino."</i>

466
00:25:53,257 --> 00:25:55,133
<i>He may look like cold iron,</i>

467
00:25:55,133 --> 00:25:57,513
<i>but inside, he's apparently
an incredibly fiery guy</i>

468
00:25:57,513 --> 00:25:59,769
who loves that kind of passionate,
go-for-broke approach.

469
00:25:59,769 --> 00:26:01,766
So that's why you provoked him?

470
00:26:01,766 --> 00:26:03,517
The fact that the company president himself
would sit in on a meeting

471
00:26:03,517 --> 00:26:05,185
with a small company
like us in the first place

472
00:26:05,185 --> 00:26:08,077
was proof that they're desperate
and grasping at straws.

473
00:26:08,077 --> 00:26:10,221
So I thought this might work.

474
00:26:10,221 --> 00:26:12,033
But still, giving them away for free?

475
00:26:12,033 --> 00:26:14,681
Hey, I was also hesitant to do that.

476
00:26:14,681 --> 00:26:19,061
But you, Mr. Seo, took it
to such an insane level.

477
00:26:19,061 --> 00:26:22,849
What, are you blaming me?
Did you just pin this on me?

478
00:26:22,849 --> 00:26:27,213
But the way you were back there
was kind of... you know...

479
00:26:27,213 --> 00:26:29,633
Well, yeah.

480
00:26:32,733 --> 00:26:34,633
Creepy!

481
00:26:38,349 --> 00:26:41,985
At any rate, there's no turning back now.

482
00:26:41,985 --> 00:26:44,100
Everything comes down to one week from now.

483
00:26:50,100 --> 00:26:54,237
Let's have faith. Let's believe in Tuesla.

484
00:26:54,237 --> 00:26:58,400
I mean, if we don't believe in it, who will?

485
00:26:59,021 --> 00:27:02,921
We can do this. I know we can!

486
00:27:10,197 --> 00:27:13,913
[Voices of Lament Over the Sealing of
Harsh Review Molco]

487
00:27:16,073 --> 00:27:17,073
[I loved Harsh Review Molco... So sad.]

488
00:27:17,073 --> 00:27:18,073
[I totally agree. I can't stop crying.]

489
00:27:18,073 --> 00:27:19,073
[They didn't have to go this far.]

490
00:27:19,073 --> 00:27:20,073
[Give them credit for apologizing properly.]

491
00:27:20,073 --> 00:27:21,773
[Awaiting your return.]

492
00:27:23,077 --> 00:27:24,147
[Honestly, the real mess
isn't Harsh Review Molco,]

493
00:27:24,147 --> 00:27:25,217
[it's Merry, isn't it?]

494
00:27:25,217 --> 00:27:27,013
[I was thinking the same thing.]

495
00:27:28,621 --> 00:27:29,621
[Merry can't be trusted.]

496
00:27:29,621 --> 00:27:31,093
[When things get tough, they just use
their official character as a shield]

497
00:27:31,093 --> 00:27:32,833
[and dump all the blame on it. So dirty.]

498
00:27:36,441 --> 00:27:39,900
I wonder if your comrades
are doing their best for us.

499
00:27:41,253 --> 00:27:44,633
I wonder if they're snagging
those additional orders.

500
00:27:46,266 --> 00:27:50,457
Well... Can you please focus?

501
00:27:50,457 --> 00:27:52,457
Sorry.

502
00:27:52,457 --> 00:27:54,205
All right, let's start over.

503
00:27:54,205 --> 00:27:57,005
It's about the design for
the mascot key holders.

504
00:27:57,005 --> 00:27:59,473
The size is 5 to 8 centimeters.

505
00:27:59,473 --> 00:28:01,637
We can focus on the details.

506
00:28:01,637 --> 00:28:05,217
A design of just the face
is also an option...

507
00:28:05,217 --> 00:28:07,113
I think it's one of those.

508
00:28:07,113 --> 00:28:09,529
In that case, just the face...

509
00:28:09,529 --> 00:28:11,337
Can you stop the performance?

510
00:28:11,337 --> 00:28:17,137
Now, Tuesla is probably
getting ironed by those iron men...

511
00:28:17,137 --> 00:28:19,637
I mean, if we don't get
those additional orders,

512
00:28:19,637 --> 00:28:21,693
we're going bankrupt, right?

513
00:28:21,693 --> 00:28:26,225
So talking about future plans is like
counting your chickens before they hatch.

514
00:28:26,225 --> 00:28:28,353
I'm back.

515
00:28:29,313 --> 00:28:30,681
I found a great place.

516
00:28:30,681 --> 00:28:32,529
A manufacturer for the mascot key holders.

517
00:28:32,529 --> 00:28:36,021
It's cheap, good quality, and we can
definitely make something cute.

518
00:28:36,021 --> 00:28:37,277
I mean, it'll definitely sell.

519
00:28:37,277 --> 00:28:40,901
Though we haven't even
finished the design yet.

520
00:28:40,901 --> 00:28:42,173
What's with you guys?

521
00:28:42,173 --> 00:28:45,233
Huh? Do I have a key holder on my face?

522
00:28:51,521 --> 00:28:53,245
Ya!

523
00:28:57,049 --> 00:28:58,800
I believe in Tuesla.

524
00:29:17,933 --> 00:29:20,001
Sorry, someone spilled coffee over here.

525
00:29:20,001 --> 00:29:21,365
Coffee?

526
00:29:21,365 --> 00:29:24,365
Well then, part-timer, can you handle it?

527
00:29:24,365 --> 00:29:26,566
Oh, okay.

528
00:29:38,549 --> 00:29:40,205
Do your best! Do your best!

529
00:29:40,205 --> 00:29:42,025
Excuse me.

530
00:29:43,637 --> 00:29:46,265
Please confirm this.

531
00:29:46,265 --> 00:29:48,333
All right, I'll check it over.

532
00:30:04,137 --> 00:30:07,445
The compliance training
execution rate is now at 100%.

533
00:30:07,445 --> 00:30:09,613
We are implementing it along with
comprehension checks.

534
00:30:09,613 --> 00:30:11,221
Understood.

535
00:30:11,221 --> 00:30:15,637
If comprehension is insufficient,
we plan to conduct additional training.

536
00:30:15,637 --> 00:30:19,233
We're also confirming the conditions
for knowledge retention.

537
00:30:21,413 --> 00:30:22,621
Are you okay?

538
00:30:22,621 --> 00:30:25,253
- Wow!
- It's a bit noisy out there.

539
00:30:26,285 --> 00:30:28,061
I'll go check.

540
00:30:36,169 --> 00:30:38,681
It's kind of hot right now.

541
00:30:38,681 --> 00:30:41,866
Wait... What are you doing here?

542
00:30:42,825 --> 00:30:44,909
Well, what are you doing here?

543
00:30:44,909 --> 00:30:48,501
I'm... on an undercover investigation,
disguised as a part-time job.

544
00:30:48,501 --> 00:30:51,657
What? So, in short, you came
because you were worried?

545
00:30:51,657 --> 00:30:53,517
- That's not it.
- No, no, no.

546
00:30:53,517 --> 00:30:56,137
You gave me so much crap about
having faith in Tuesla,

547
00:30:56,137 --> 00:30:57,661
and yet you don't trust it at all.

548
00:30:57,661 --> 00:30:59,877
- You're just like me.
- No, I'm completely different.

549
00:30:59,877 --> 00:31:01,973
I just came to see them in action.

550
00:31:01,973 --> 00:31:04,866
I just came to see Tuesla's brilliant work,
which I never doubted.

551
00:31:04,866 --> 00:31:07,917
"In action"? You were so worried
you couldn't stand it...

552
00:31:18,841 --> 00:31:20,457
If this keeps up,

553
00:31:20,457 --> 00:31:23,000
- and there's no effect...
- It's fine.

554
00:31:24,449 --> 00:31:27,166
Let's believe in Tuesla.

555
00:31:27,166 --> 00:31:29,273
It's not so easy to just do that.

556
00:31:36,177 --> 00:31:39,561
Core, core, core.

557
00:31:39,561 --> 00:31:41,593
Core, core, core, core, core...

558
00:31:41,593 --> 00:31:44,321
- Part-timer, we're heading out now.
- Okay.

559
00:31:44,321 --> 00:31:45,817
See you.

560
00:31:45,817 --> 00:31:47,621
- Wow!
- Oh!

561
00:31:47,621 --> 00:31:49,737
- Are you okay?
- I'm fine.

562
00:31:49,737 --> 00:31:52,165
- I just got a little leg cramp.
- Is it bad?

563
00:31:52,165 --> 00:31:53,650
- Can you move? Are you okay?
- I'm fine. Well, it's just a cramp.

564
00:31:53,650 --> 00:31:56,265
I'm fine. Let's go. Okay.

565
00:31:56,265 --> 00:31:57,981
I knew it. It's a bit...

566
00:32:02,445 --> 00:32:05,166
Huh? My keys...

567
00:32:07,957 --> 00:32:10,285
They're gone. My keys are gone!

568
00:32:10,285 --> 00:32:13,329
Gone! No way! My keys are gone!

569
00:32:21,645 --> 00:32:25,409
[Mizuki Seo: I forgot my keys.
I'm going to go get them.]

570
00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:54,969
<i>♪ I want to sleep, but I can't ♪</i>

571
00:32:54,969 --> 00:32:59,725
<i>♪ I'm so anxious. My eyes are wide open ♪</i>

572
00:32:59,725 --> 00:33:04,729
<i>♪ Even if I shout that I believe ♪</i>

573
00:33:04,729 --> 00:33:10,749
<i>♪ I'm so anxious that I get a leg cramp ♪</i>

574
00:33:10,749 --> 00:33:15,228
<i>♪ That's me ♪</i>

575
00:33:15,228 --> 00:33:20,093
<i>♪ The grim reaper girl ♪</i>

576
00:33:20,093 --> 00:33:24,345
<i>♪ Bringing bankruptcy ♪</i>

577
00:33:24,345 --> 00:33:28,966
- <i>♪ The grim reaper girl ♪</i>
- O-Con.

578
00:33:31,629 --> 00:33:34,581
Wow! What are you doing here?

579
00:33:34,581 --> 00:33:36,573
Are you okay?

580
00:33:36,573 --> 00:33:38,781
Wait, did you see that? You saw it, right?

581
00:33:38,781 --> 00:33:40,933
You saw it, so you're asking
if I'm okay, right?

582
00:33:40,933 --> 00:33:43,766
- I saw it.
- Then I'm not okay!

583
00:33:43,766 --> 00:33:45,445
No one would be okay
after being seen like this!

584
00:33:45,445 --> 00:33:46,873
I can't go on living!

585
00:33:46,873 --> 00:33:49,633
Night after night, singing songs
I made up a cappella in the closet...

586
00:33:49,633 --> 00:33:52,621
- That aside...
- It's not "that aside"!

587
00:33:52,621 --> 00:33:54,137
Right now, that's all there is to talk about!

588
00:33:54,137 --> 00:33:56,349
For me right now, there's nothing else
but being seen doing that!

589
00:33:56,349 --> 00:33:58,145
More importantly, are you okay?

590
00:33:58,145 --> 00:33:59,921
You mean that "are you okay?"

591
00:33:59,921 --> 00:34:02,597
You're asking me if I'm crazy, right?

592
00:34:02,597 --> 00:34:05,600
You're asking me,
the Midnight Closet A Cappella Woman,

593
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,753
if I'm crazy, right?

594
00:34:07,753 --> 00:34:08,933
- Right?
- No, no.

595
00:34:08,933 --> 00:34:12,053
The Midnight Closet A Cappella Woman,
that's what she is like.

596
00:34:12,053 --> 00:34:13,609
- More importantly, that song...
- "That's what she is like"?

597
00:34:13,609 --> 00:34:15,121
No, no. Don't let your guard down!

598
00:34:15,121 --> 00:34:17,166
It's the Midnight Closet A Cappella Woman.

599
00:34:17,166 --> 00:34:19,497
Doesn't it sound like
an urban legend monster?

600
00:34:19,497 --> 00:34:20,865
If you just end it with
"that's what she is like,"

601
00:34:20,865 --> 00:34:23,115
she'll eat you if you
meet her on a dark street.

602
00:34:23,115 --> 00:34:25,723
I'm saying! Forget it.
That song from earlier. You can't sleep?

603
00:34:25,723 --> 00:34:28,245
- Your leg cramps from anxiety?
- No! Don't say it!

604
00:34:28,245 --> 00:34:30,625
I don't want to hear it! I've hit my limit!

605
00:34:39,385 --> 00:34:43,109
[All Japan Heavy Industries]

606
00:34:45,861 --> 00:34:47,877
This day has finally come...

607
00:34:51,253 --> 00:34:55,361
Hey, you haven't told anyone
about that, right? Right?

608
00:34:55,361 --> 00:34:58,721
Because if you did, then on a dark street...

609
00:35:03,797 --> 00:35:07,117
Welcome.

610
00:35:07,117 --> 00:35:10,893
- Huh?
- Oh, my! Kusakabe?

611
00:35:10,893 --> 00:35:12,609
You came here too, Ms. Kominami?

612
00:35:12,609 --> 00:35:13,921
Yeah, I did. I did.

613
00:35:13,921 --> 00:35:16,353
I'm pretty tight with this place.

614
00:35:16,353 --> 00:35:19,089
By the way, among the people here,

615
00:35:19,089 --> 00:35:21,525
I think there may have been a few of them
who had this little guy here.

616
00:35:21,525 --> 00:35:24,165
Did you notice any kind of pleasant vibe
drifting around them?

617
00:35:24,165 --> 00:35:25,833
Did you see anything like that?

618
00:35:25,833 --> 00:35:29,185
No, wait, wait.
There weren't any plushies at all.

619
00:35:29,185 --> 00:35:32,017
What? No, that can't be...

620
00:35:32,017 --> 00:35:35,566
We mean... the executives. The big bosses.

621
00:35:35,566 --> 00:35:39,893
Yeah. I went around to those
high-and-mighty folks doing my thing,

622
00:35:39,893 --> 00:35:43,665
but the number of people
with a plushie was zero. Zero.

623
00:35:43,665 --> 00:35:46,789
- Bye. Want a drink?
- Oh, I'm fine.

624
00:35:49,337 --> 00:35:52,533
Don't tell me they threw them away
because they didn't work?

625
00:35:56,973 --> 00:35:58,866
- It's fine...
- I believe.

626
00:36:00,117 --> 00:36:02,100
I believe in Tuesla.

627
00:36:03,777 --> 00:36:07,400
I mean, if we don't believe in it, who will?

628
00:36:10,321 --> 00:36:12,113
We can do this.

629
00:36:13,221 --> 00:36:15,097
I know we can.

630
00:36:21,461 --> 00:36:23,900
It really does feel different...

631
00:36:26,457 --> 00:36:28,533
- Did his leg just cramp up?
- It hurts!

632
00:36:29,725 --> 00:36:33,533
We can do this. I know we can!

633
00:36:34,861 --> 00:36:37,321
I don't think it feels any different.

634
00:36:38,361 --> 00:36:42,333
Mr. Seo probably...

635
00:36:48,701 --> 00:36:50,437
Let's go.

636
00:36:57,873 --> 00:37:03,233
- Well, about Tuesla...
- Before we get to that, I have a report.

637
00:37:04,961 --> 00:37:07,541
As promised,

638
00:37:07,541 --> 00:37:13,009
we kept the Tutu-Tuesla plushies
close to us at all times.

639
00:37:13,009 --> 00:37:14,921
However,

640
00:37:14,921 --> 00:37:21,173
there wasn't anything
that could be called a dramatic change.

641
00:37:25,489 --> 00:37:27,466
But yesterday...

642
00:37:28,741 --> 00:37:32,400
<i>When I was in the lounge,</i>

643
00:37:32,400 --> 00:37:34,237
- <i>a new employee came up to me</i>
- That plushie is cute.

644
00:37:34,237 --> 00:37:35,441
<i>and spoke to me.</i>

645
00:37:35,441 --> 00:37:38,697
- Oh, I see.
- <i>She said it was cute.</i>

646
00:37:38,697 --> 00:37:42,413
<i>From there, we had some casual chit-chat,</i>

647
00:37:42,413 --> 00:37:47,765
and then she shared some ideas she had
for reforming our department.

648
00:37:48,569 --> 00:37:52,637
Those ideas were quite interesting.

649
00:37:53,273 --> 00:37:56,665
It made me think that
if it weren't for Tuesla,

650
00:37:56,665 --> 00:38:01,333
these ideas would never
have seen the light of day.

651
00:38:02,285 --> 00:38:05,417
We've been focusing on
monthly harassment training

652
00:38:05,417 --> 00:38:08,785
and stress management.

653
00:38:08,785 --> 00:38:14,033
But more than that, making it easier
for people to speak up to their superiors,

654
00:38:14,033 --> 00:38:19,677
or just having casual chats,
such simple things are the most effective.

655
00:38:19,677 --> 00:38:22,266
I hadn't even noticed that before.

656
00:38:23,777 --> 00:38:28,745
Maybe Tuesla opened that door for us.

657
00:38:28,745 --> 00:38:31,421
But why isn't it anywhere?

658
00:38:31,421 --> 00:38:35,657
Actually, our Osaka branch has a high
turnover rate among young employees,

659
00:38:35,657 --> 00:38:38,253
which has been a pressing issue for us.

660
00:38:38,253 --> 00:38:42,253
So yesterday afternoon,
we sent all the Tueslas

661
00:38:42,253 --> 00:38:45,197
to the Osaka branch.

662
00:38:45,197 --> 00:38:48,077
And so,

663
00:38:48,077 --> 00:38:53,089
would it be all right if we placed
an additional order for Tutu-Tuesla?

664
00:38:55,261 --> 00:38:57,257
Of course.

665
00:39:03,749 --> 00:39:06,837
All right, then we'll take 1,000 of them.

666
00:39:07,745 --> 00:39:08,849
A thousand...

667
00:39:08,849 --> 00:39:11,773
No, no, sir. You must have
the wrong number of zeros.

668
00:39:11,773 --> 00:39:13,877
- Hold on.
- Could you do it

669
00:39:13,877 --> 00:39:16,169
as quickly as you can?

670
00:39:16,169 --> 00:39:20,405
It's just that... now that they're gone,
we can't help but...

671
00:39:25,897 --> 00:39:27,733
Understood.

672
00:39:28,349 --> 00:39:32,213
We'll treat this as our top priority.

673
00:39:35,433 --> 00:39:38,457
Well, excuse me.

674
00:39:38,457 --> 00:39:41,833
But may I borrow that for a moment?

675
00:39:42,757 --> 00:39:44,466
Of course.

676
00:39:50,121 --> 00:39:53,481
Hey, let me have that too!

677
00:39:53,481 --> 00:39:56,866
Don't panic! Don't panic!

678
00:39:57,597 --> 00:40:02,368
At first, I thought that
any plushie would do.

679
00:40:02,368 --> 00:40:04,733
But they taught me that's not true.

680
00:40:06,877 --> 00:40:09,637
Hyenas are usually seen as disliked,

681
00:40:09,637 --> 00:40:12,533
but the fact that it cuddles up cutely
is exactly why it works.

682
00:40:13,309 --> 00:40:18,045
Well, we've also suffered from
images and stereotypes ourselves.

683
00:40:18,045 --> 00:40:20,893
Shouldn't you touch it too, Mr. Hino?

684
00:40:20,893 --> 00:40:24,033
What are you talking about?
I'm Blast Furnace Hino!

685
00:40:24,033 --> 00:40:28,545
Enough of that. Look, it's about to leave.

686
00:40:29,393 --> 00:40:31,185
Jeez.

687
00:40:34,977 --> 00:40:36,709
It's fine.

688
00:40:41,781 --> 00:40:43,681
I love it.

689
00:40:43,681 --> 00:40:46,485
Thank you for your business.
We really appreciate it.

690
00:40:46,485 --> 00:40:47,800
Thank you.

691
00:40:47,800 --> 00:40:49,415
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

692
00:40:49,415 --> 00:40:51,215
As for what's to come...

693
00:40:56,249 --> 00:40:58,309
Is something wrong?

694
00:40:58,309 --> 00:41:00,093
Oh, no.

695
00:41:03,053 --> 00:41:04,865
It's just...

696
00:41:06,229 --> 00:41:11,333
I wish I could have shown that scene
to my childhood self.

697
00:41:13,389 --> 00:41:15,785
<i>All that stuff about it being weird
for men to like cute things,</i>

698
00:41:15,785 --> 00:41:17,905
<i>Tuesla just blows it all away.</i>

699
00:41:17,905 --> 00:41:20,149
<i>That's what I was thinking.</i>

700
00:41:24,825 --> 00:41:26,857
The one who blew it away...

701
00:41:28,933 --> 00:41:31,733
is you, who created this character.

702
00:41:35,137 --> 00:41:37,045
Let's keep showing them.

703
00:41:37,785 --> 00:41:39,657
Scenes like that.

704
00:41:42,533 --> 00:41:44,773
To everyone who can't
put their love into words.

705
00:41:46,821 --> 00:41:48,793
To all of them.

706
00:41:59,317 --> 00:42:01,800
- Hey, O-Con...
- Oh, right!

707
00:42:03,401 --> 00:42:06,093
Mr. Hino! Mr. Hino!

708
00:42:06,093 --> 00:42:10,766
Actually, we're working on
some new Tuesla merchandise.

709
00:42:10,766 --> 00:42:13,825
- Oh?
- This is the keychain.

710
00:42:13,825 --> 00:42:15,505
- A keychain?
- Yes.

711
00:42:15,505 --> 00:42:19,857
Plushies are always there for you,
but something like a keychain,

712
00:42:19,857 --> 00:42:24,393
if you carry it in your pocket,
it becomes like a good luck charm.

713
00:42:27,097 --> 00:42:30,037
Here are the sales figures
for Docchicchi's first week.

714
00:42:30,037 --> 00:42:32,156
[Merry is the worst!]
The boycott on social media

715
00:42:32,156 --> 00:42:34,266
[Don't buy Docchicchi!]
had a huge impact.

716
00:42:34,909 --> 00:42:37,800
[Omishima totally deserved it.]
There are many plushies being returned...

717
00:42:37,800 --> 00:42:38,800
[Omishima's blunder is one
for the history books.]

718
00:42:38,800 --> 00:42:40,541
I'm very sorry.

719
00:42:41,273 --> 00:42:43,253
[I won't pay money to a company
that makes fun of people!]

720
00:42:43,253 --> 00:42:46,037
[Congratulations on
the major flop of Docchicchi.]

721
00:42:48,600 --> 00:42:50,821
[Compared to the Merry boycott,
this is fairly peaceful news.]

722
00:42:50,821 --> 00:42:52,477
[Omishima should take notes.]

723
00:42:52,477 --> 00:42:53,553
[All Japan Heavy Industries Announces
New Internal Reforms,]

724
00:42:53,553 --> 00:42:54,753
[Handing Out 1,000 Plushies to Employees]

725
00:42:54,753 --> 00:42:58,765
[Mr. Hino Holding a Tutu-Tuesla Plushie]

726
00:43:01,741 --> 00:43:04,173
I'm on it.

727
00:43:06,141 --> 00:43:08,697
It's good that the repairs were done quickly.

728
00:43:08,697 --> 00:43:11,361
All right, thanks for waiting.

729
00:43:11,361 --> 00:43:13,333
Switch on!

730
00:43:27,233 --> 00:43:29,593
Electric fans are so retro, aren't they?

731
00:43:29,593 --> 00:43:32,657
It was here before I moved in.

732
00:43:32,657 --> 00:43:35,453
This is your grandma's house, right, Mr. Seo?

733
00:43:35,453 --> 00:43:38,537
Oh, the same grandma
with those quirky sayings?

734
00:43:38,537 --> 00:43:41,045
Sorry about that.

735
00:43:41,045 --> 00:43:43,625
No, I... I'm not complaining.

736
00:43:43,625 --> 00:43:46,285
Just saying they're quirky.

737
00:43:46,285 --> 00:43:50,141
Well... if that's the case, how is she now?

738
00:43:50,141 --> 00:43:53,549
Huh? She's alive and well.

739
00:43:53,549 --> 00:43:57,369
Mr. Seo's grandma,
she must be around 80, right?

740
00:43:57,369 --> 00:43:59,753
78! How rude.

741
00:43:59,753 --> 00:44:01,725
Oh, sorry.

742
00:44:04,341 --> 00:44:07,445
Zapaan.

743
00:44:07,445 --> 00:44:09,366
Zapaan?

744
00:44:13,353 --> 00:44:15,421
What are you doing here, Ms. Mondo?

745
00:44:15,421 --> 00:44:18,153
What do you mean?

746
00:44:18,153 --> 00:44:21,773
This is my house, so of course I'm here.

747
00:44:21,773 --> 00:44:24,057
What? But this is...

748
00:44:24,057 --> 00:44:26,489
Huh? What?

749
00:44:26,489 --> 00:44:28,473
You're the grandma?

750
00:44:34,897 --> 00:44:38,300
Right, Mi?

751
00:44:39,441 --> 00:44:42,166
Mi?

752
00:44:45,273 --> 00:44:49,213
- What?
- What?
